\newlabel{tab:h1results}{{3}{15}{H1 results on the same-template control and the exact $167/80$ target. Confidence intervals are bootstrap $95\%$ intervals over the deterministic seed panel.\relax }{table.caption.6}{}}
\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {4}{\ignorespaces H2 results. The ladder goal-hit intervals are Wilson $95\%$ intervals. The real order-$668$ packet has $N=1$, so the deterministic values are reported directly.\relax }}{15}{table.caption.9}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:h2results}{{4}{15}{H2 results. The ladder goal-hit intervals are Wilson $95\%$ intervals. The real order-$668$ packet has $N=1$, so the deterministic values are reported directly.\relax }{table.caption.9}{}}
\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {section}{\numberline {8}Discussion}{15}{section.8}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{sec:discussion}{{8}{15}{Discussion}{section.8}{}}
\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {subsection}{\numberline {8.1}What failed and why that failure is informative}{15}{subsection.8.1}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4}{\ignorespaces Main H1 quantitative comparisons. Top row: mean best distance to the exact target with bootstrap $95\%$ confidence intervals for the control and the target sweeps. Bottom row: paired seed-level comparisons between \texttt  {parallel\_gain\_ca}{} and \texttt  {direct\_greedy}{}. On the control, the baseline wins a clear majority of seeds even though neither method solves exactly. On the target, every paired comparison favors \texttt  {direct\_greedy}{} on the primary metric. Because Propositions\nobreakspace  {}\ref {prop:h1connect}--\ref {prop:h1fair} show that the state space, neighborhood, objective, and move cap are shared, these losses cannot be explained away as benchmark mismatch.\relax }}{16}{figure.caption.7}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:h1-results}{{4}{16}{Main H1 quantitative comparisons. Top row: mean best distance to the exact target with bootstrap $95\%$ confidence intervals for the control and the target sweeps. Bottom row: paired seed-level comparisons between \CA {} and \DG {}. On the control, the baseline wins a clear majority of seeds even though neither method solves exactly. On the target, every paired comparison favors \DG {} on the primary metric. Because Propositions~\ref {prop:h1connect}--\ref {prop:h1fair} show that the state space, neighborhood, objective, and move cap are shared, these losses cannot be explained away as benchmark mismatch.\relax }{figure.caption.7}{}}
\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {subsection}{\numberline {8.2}Benchmark limits}{16}{subsection.8.2}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {5}{\ignorespaces H1 reachability and timing diagnostics. Panel (a) reports orbit summaries for both packets: the serious CA rule visits far fewer canonical orbits than the matched baseline, while the negative controls diffuse broadly but do not achieve good objective values. Panels (b)--(c) show empirical CDFs of the step at which the best recorded state is first achieved. The CA branch peaks much earlier than the baseline on both packets, especially on the exact target, which is consistent with the repository's interpretation of a reachability collapse rather than with a hidden long-horizon advantage.\relax }}{17}{figure.caption.8}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:h1-dynamics}{{5}{17}{H1 reachability and timing diagnostics. Panel (a) reports orbit summaries for both packets: the serious CA rule visits far fewer canonical orbits than the matched baseline, while the negative controls diffuse broadly but do not achieve good objective values. Panels (b)--(c) show empirical CDFs of the step at which the best recorded state is first achieved. The CA branch peaks much earlier than the baseline on both packets, especially on the exact target, which is consistent with the repository's interpretation of a reachability collapse rather than with a hidden long-horizon advantage.\relax }{figure.caption.8}{}}
\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {subsection}{\numberline {8.3}The status of the literal ``cellar'' branch}{17}{subsection.8.3}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {subsection}{\numberline {8.4}Societal and scientific implications}{17}{subsection.8.4}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {6}{\ignorespaces H2 mechanism and results. (a) The structured $(q,s)$ state induces four derived sequences whose combined aperiodic defect vector defines the modular objective. (b) The toy $n=9$ ladder is a weak discriminator because random controls solve most starts; the wide Wilson intervals reflect the tiny panel size. (c) On the only real degraded order-$668$ start, \texttt  {parallel\_gain\_ca}{} and \texttt  {direct\_greedy}{} tie exactly on the best attained $\ell _1$ defect and both regress to the same final state. (d) Weak controls visit many more orbits, but that coverage is not useful because it is achieved by sacrificing the registered modular objective.\relax }}{18}{figure.caption.10}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:h2-results}{{6}{18}{H2 mechanism and results. (a) The structured $(q,s)$ state induces four derived sequences whose combined aperiodic defect vector defines the modular objective. (b) The toy $n=9$ ladder is a weak discriminator because random controls solve most starts; the wide Wilson intervals reflect the tiny panel size. (c) On the only real degraded order-$668$ start, \CA {} and \DG {} tie exactly on the best attained $\ell _1$ defect and both regress to the same final state. (d) Weak controls visit many more orbits, but that coverage is not useful because it is achieved by sacrificing the registered modular objective.\relax }{figure.caption.10}{}}
\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {section}{\numberline {9}Conclusion}{18}{section.9}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {7}{\ignorespaces Executed branches versus reserve branches. Both executed branches have exact anchors, were run under matched baselines, and produced no positive result. The literal cellar / pushdown reserve and the convolution-slice reserve remain interesting only because they change the state representation materially. They do not inherit any empirical credit from the failed H1/H2 packet.\relax }}{19}{figure.caption.11}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:branch-matrix}{{7}{19}{Executed branches versus reserve branches. Both executed branches have exact anchors, were run under matched baselines, and produced no positive result. The literal cellar / pushdown reserve and the convolution-slice reserve remain interesting only because they change the state representation materially. They do not inherit any empirical credit from the failed H1/H2 packet.\relax }{figure.caption.11}{}}
